      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 that have distinct digits. We can begin by breaking down the problem into several cases based on the hundreds digit (the rightmost digit) being odd and even. \n\n### Case 1: Hundreds Digit is 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9 (Odd Case)\n\n#### 1. When the hundreds digit is 1:\n- The tens digit can take any value except 1 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (3, 5, 7, 9).\n- The units digit can take any value except 1 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (0, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n\nTherefore, we have \\(5 \\times 5 = 25\\) odd numbers in this case.\n\n#### 2. When the hundreds digit is 3:\n- The tens digit can take any value except 3 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (1, 5, 7, 9).\n- The units digit can take any value except 3 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (0, 1, 5, 7, 9).\n\nTherefore, we have \\(5 \\times 5 = 25\\) odd numbers in this case.\n\n#### 3. When the hundreds digit is 5:\n- The tens digit can take any value except 5 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (1, 3, 7, 9).\n- The units digit can take any value except 5 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (0, 1, 3, 7, 9).\n\nTherefore, we have \\(5 \\times 5 = 25\\) odd numbers in this case.\n\n#### 4. When the hundreds digit is 7:\n- The tens digit can take any value except 7 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (1, 3, 5, 9).\n- The units digit can take any value except 7 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (0, 1, 3, 5, 9).\n\nTherefore, we have \\(5 \\times 5 = 25\\) odd numbers in this case.\n\n#### 5. When the hundreds digit is 9:\n- The tens digit can take any value except 9 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (1, 3, 5, 7).\n- The units digit can take any value except 9 (since it must be odd), which gives us 5 choices (0, 1, 3, 5, 7).\n\nTherefore, we have \\(5 \\times 5 = 25\\) odd numbers in this case.\n\nAdding up the numbers from all five cases, we get \\(25 + 25 + 25 + 25 + 25 = 125\\). \n\nHence, we conclude that the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 that have distinct digits is \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
